"[2601.07182v1] PRPO: Aligning Process Reward with Outcome Reward in Policy Optimization (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07182v1)\nciteturn26941view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07182v1\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 168\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Jan 2026 (this version), latest version 3 Feb 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:PRPO: Aligning Process Reward with Outcome Reward in Policy Optimization\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ruiyi Ding , cite7†Yongxuan Lv , cite8†Xianhui Meng , cite9†Jiahe Song , cite10†Chao Wang , cite11†Chen Jiang , cite12†Yuan Cheng L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled PRPO: Aligning Process Reward with Outcome Reward in Policy Optimization, by Ruiyi Ding and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Policy optimization for large language models often suffers from sparse reward signals in multi-step reasoning tasks. Critic-free methods like GRPO assign a single normalized outcome reward to all tokens, providing limited guidance for intermediate reasoning . While Process Reward Models (PRMs) offer dense feedback, they risk premature collapse when used alone, as early low-reward tokens can drive policies toward truncated outputs.\nL17: We introduce Process Relative Policy Optimization (PRPO), which combines outcome reliability with process-level guidance in a critic-free framework. PRPO segments reasoning sequences based on semantic clues, normalizes PRM scores into token-level advantages, and aligns their distribution with outcome advantages through location-parameter shift.\nL18: On MATH500, PRPO improves Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B accuracy from 61.2% to 64.4% over GRPO using only eight rollouts and no value network, demonstrating efficient fine-grained credit assignment within critic-free optimization.\nL19: Comments:  | 8 pages, 2 figures\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | I.2.6; I.2.7\nL22: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2601.07182 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2601.07182v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.07182†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Ruiyi Ding [cite18†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 12 Jan 2026 04:04:43 UTC (5,875 KB)\nL29: cite19†[v2] Tue, 13 Jan 2026 07:35:53 UTC (5,875 KB)\nL30: cite5†[v3] Tue, 3 Feb 2026 09:02:21 UTC (5,915 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled PRPO: Aligning Process Reward with Outcome Reward in Policy Optimization, by Ruiyi Ding and 6 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite13†View PDF L39:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite20†TeX Source L41: \nL42: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.LG\nL46: \nL47: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L48: \nL49: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2026-01 L50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: cite27†cs L54: cite28†cs.AI L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite31†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL63: \nL64: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL65: \nL66: [Button: ×]\nL67: \nL68: loading...\nL69: \nL70: Data provided by:\nL71: \nL72: ### Bookmark\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL75: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL